WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.180
Stack discipline is essential in memory-constrained C because stack use can grow quietly.

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:17.240
A function that looks harmless in isolation may call formatting code, validation helpers, error handlers, and driver wrappers that add more local storage and call depth than expected.

3
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.780
The risk is highest when unusual paths are not measured.

4
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:28.500
Stack sizing should consider normal paths, error paths, cleanup paths, and interrupt-like conditions.

5
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:34.760
A parser may use little stack for valid input but more stack when reporting malformed input.

6
00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.380
A diagnostic function may allocate temporary formatting buffers.

7
00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.040
A retry path may call code that the normal path does not.

8
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.960
Recursion is often risky because it makes stack use harder to bound.

9
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:54.780
Some algorithms can be rewritten iteratively or with an explicit bounded work queue.

10
00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:01.600
If recursion is used, the maximum depth should be tied to a validated input limit and reviewed as part of the memory budget.

11
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.440
Large local objects need careful review.

12
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:10.860
A local array can be convenient, but it consumes stack every time the function is active.

13
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:22.000
If the object is large, long-lived, shared across calls, or required only for rare cases, static storage, caller-owned storage, or a controlled pool may be more predictable.

14
00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:28.440
Interrupt stacks and task stacks need separate budgets in RTOS-like or device-facing systems.

15
00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:37.660
A task stack sized for normal operation may not account for nested callbacks, interrupt preemption, logging, or library calls.

16
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.280
Each execution context should have a clear stack assumption.

17
00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.120
Stack high-water marks and static analysis can help validate assumptions.

18
00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:52.140
They should be used with tests that exercise worst-case messages, error handling, and configured features.

19
00:01:52.240 --> 00:02:00.560
Reviewers should ask what the deepest call path is, what local objects exist on that path, and whether the measurement matches the release build.
